# ---

# NOTES

# This document assumes a little-endian system with byte-addressable memory.
# Semantics will remain undefined for big-endian systems and addressable units
# other than a byte until an actual need to run in one of these situations
# arises, although changing the size of an addressable unit is likely the
# better defined of the two. The ordering will certainly be different on a
# big-endian system, but hopefully type definitions are expressive enough that
# it should only require adjusting the type specifications.

# ---

# This is a comment.
# Semicolons end most statements. Blocks are terminated with `end`.

# The bit is the fundamental type. A variable definition is a type name
# followed by the variable name:

bit enabled;

# At the start of compilation, `bit` is one of the only named types available
# to variables. We can name new types with the `type` keyword, followed by an
# alias name and a type specifier. Square brackets [ ] indicate a type
# specifier. Types are composites of other types. All values reduce to raw bits.
# The simplest type specifier is a multiple of another type:

type nibb [4 bit];
type byte [8 bit];  # or [2 nibb]
type half [2 byte]; # or [16 bit]

# This makes the name `byte` an alias for `[8 bit]`. These variables are the
# same type:

byte     count;
[8 bit]  count1;

# Numeric literals can be prefixed with `$` for hex and `%` for binary. Only
# decimal (base 10) values may be prefixed with a `-` to be negative. Negative
# numeric literals are shorthand for their two's complement representation (the
# exact width depends on the expected type at that location). Literals have no
# implicit type. This is a valid definition for a sixteen byte type:

type sixteen [$10 byte];

# The top level of a program contains definitions. A function definition looks
# like a variable definition, but the name is immediately followed by an open
# parenthesis. The type indicates the function's return type, as in C.
# Here is a trivial function that accepts no arguments and returns a byte:

byte get_count()
    return count;
end

# The special type name `void` indicates the lack of a type. Functions can be
# void, but not variables. A void function may not return a value, but may use
# `return` with no value to exit the function. Variable assignments and math
# expressions work as expected:

void increase_count(byte val)
    count = count + val;
end

# As before, named types are aliases for their type specifier:

[8 bit] get_inc_count([1 byte] val)
    [2 nibb] c = count;
    count = count + val;
    return c;
end

# All operators assume unsigned values, including comparison. While an
# expression is being evaluated, its operands are promoted to the widest type
# participating in the expression (including the target) by zero-padding the
# most significant bits. Conversions to a narrower bit-width keep the least
# significant bits and truncate the rest. The `signed` built-in changes this
# behavior to sign-extend instead of zero-padding for an operand:

half sub_i8(half lhs, byte rhs)
    return lhs - signed(rhs);
end

byte rshift_logic(byte val)
    return val >> 1;
end

byte rshift_arith(byte val)
    [9 bit] tmp = signed(val);
    return tmp >> 1;
end

# We can use the `as` keyword to cast, avoiding the temporary variable:

byte rshift_arith1(byte val)
    return signed(val) as [9 bit] >> 1;
end

# Two other built-ins are `bits` and `size`. They accept a type name or
# variable name, similar to `sizeof` in C. `bits` returns the width of a type
# or variable in bits (ignoring leading alignment). `size` returns the number
# of addressable units (bytes) required to store a value or type. All built-ins
# use the same syntax as a function call; the name, followed by the argument in
# parentheses.

# Type specifications can be more advanced than just a count of another type.
# We'll call each part of a type a "component". `[8 bit]` has eight components,
# each one a bit. `[2 nibb]` has two components, each a nibb. Types can assign
# names to their components with dot notation:

type point8 [
    1 byte .x 
    1 byte .y 
];

point8 as_point(byte x, byte y)
    point8 result;
    result.x = x;
    result.y = y;
    return result;
end

# This is valid, and will produce the same result as above for little-endian:

point8 as_point_manual(byte x, byte y)
    half result;
    result = y << 8;
    return result | x;
end

# Components are stored in their listed order, with earlier components having
# lower memory addresses, up to the addressable unit of the target system
# (assumed to be a byte). Within an addressable unit, components are stored
# starting at the least-significant bit. Within a type, no padding is added by
# default. A type can specify padding with two special type names, `pad` and
# `align`. Here are two ways to describe a byte-sized boolean. There are subtle
# differences between these definitions; see below.

type bool    [1 bit 7 pad];
type boolean [8 align 1 bit 8 align];

# `pad` will add exactly that many bits of padding. `align` will pad to the
# next multiple of the given number of bits. Modifications to a value with a
# padded type are masked to ensure padding bits are zero, allowing direct
# comparison. When `align` is the first component of a top-level type
# specifier, it aligns the type to that boundary but is not part of the storage
# of that type. Put another way, size([X align, 1 Y]) == size(Y) when not
# nested in another type specifier. `pad`, on the other hand, is always included
# in the type's storage. Note that variable definitions are aligned to the next
# addressable unit by default:

[1 bit] flag_a;
[1 bit] flag_b;

# If these flags need to live in the same addressable unit, they need to be
# part of the same type specifier. As written this would place each variable in
# its own addressable unit, otherwise it wouldn't be possible to take their
# address. To align them more coarsely, write:

[32 align 1 bool]    flag_c;
[32 align 1 boolean] flag_d;

# The above boolean examples behave differently in a few edge-cases:

type example9   [1 bit 1 bool];    # This is a 9 bit type
type example12  [4 bit 1 bool];    # This is a 12 bit type
type example16a [1 bit 1 boolean]; # This is a 16 bit type
type example16b [4 bit 1 boolean]; # This is also a 16 bit type

# The `[1 bit 7 pad]` type specifier always takes exactly 8 bits, and does not
# impose any limitations on its alignment. When used in another type specifier,
# it simply takes up 8 bits wherever it appears. The `[8 align 1 bit 8 align]`
# type specifier takes up a variable number of bits depending on its placement
# in a containing type specifier; it could be as small as 8 or as large as 15.
# Multiple leading `align`s will reduce to a single compatible value, if one
# exists, otherwise it is an error.

# Named components can be nested:

type rect8 [
    1 point8 .pos
    1 point8 .size
];

half area(rect8 r)
    half result = r.size.x;
    return result * r.size.y;
end

# Components can also be accessed by indexing with square brackets:

# This assumes byte is defined as [8 bit]
bit bit_indexing(byte x, byte idx)
    return x[idx]; # Index is in multiples of `bit`
end

# Recall that half is [2 byte]
byte byte_indexing(half x, bit idx)
    return x[idx]; # Index is in multiples of `byte`
end

# This gives us the language's way of defining an array:

[32 rect8] boxes;

# Bounds are not checked. This works through named components as well. It is an
# error to attempt to index a type whose components are different sizes:

type color8 [
    1 byte .r
    1 byte .g
    1 byte .b
];
type color_rect [
    1 color8 .color
    1 rect8  .rect
];

void indexing_example()
    rect8 r;
    r[0] = $0201; # Ok: r.pos = ...
    r[1] = $0403; # Ok: r.size = ...
    color_rect cr; 
    # cr[0] = $FF00FF; # Not ok: bits(cr.color) != bits(cr.rect)
    cr.color[0] = $FF; # Ok: cr.color.r = ...
    cr.color = $FF00FF; # Also ok
end

# The `len` built-in can be used on any indexable type to get the number of
# indexable components. Think of it like C's `sizeof(buf)/sizeof(buf[0])`.

# The included flow-control constructs should be familiar. The parentheses
# around the condition are required. There is no for loop; it can be written as
# a `while` loop.

void flow_examples(bit condition, byte num)
    if (condition) # 0 is falsey, anything else is true
        ; # no-op statement
    elif (num > 0)
        goto after;
    else
        ;
    end

after:
    while (condition)
        ;
    end
end

# The `break` and `continue` keywords may be used in the body of a `while` loop
# with their usual semantics. `goto` labels must be function-local. There is a
# special variant of `goto` intended for use where a switch statement or
# computed goto is needed called `dispatch`:

byte dispatch_example(byte i)

    dispatch op, i;

    # Errors fall through
    return 255;

op0:
    return 0;
op1:
    return 1;
op2:
    return 2;
op3:
    return 3;
end

# `dispatch` takes a label prefix and a value. If a label exists with the
# prefix and appended base 10 value (see example), the program jumps to that
# label. If no label exists for the value the statement is a no-op and control
# falls through to the code immediately below it. There is a limit of 256
# labels for a single `dispatch` statement, and the index is [8 bit].

# There is one additional built-in type aside from `bit`. `address` is a type
# whose width is the size of a pointer on the target system. The address of a
# variables or function is obtained with a `@` prefix: `@foo`. The components
# of a type may also be addressed as long as they are guaranteed to be aligned
# to an addressable unit: `@r.color[1]`. A reference associates a type with
# an address. A reference looks like a variable definition, but the type is
# prefixed with `@` and the name is followed by the `is` keyword, as shown in
# the example below. A reference is essentially a variable whose storage
# location is provided by the programmer. Defining a reference allocates no
# space. Function arguments cannot be defined as references. Use an address
# instead. References must be given an address where they are defined and this
# address is fixed for the lifetime/scope of the reference. The contents of a
# plain address can be assigned with the `@=` operator; the type of the
# target is the type of the assigned value. Assignment through a reference
# uses `=` for the same effect.  `@=` is a double indirection on a register
# (address stored at an address).


void by_ref_example_1(address a)
    @rect8 r is a; # Define a reference. Now we can refer to `r`.
    address a2 = @r; # @r == a
    rect8 r2 = r; # Value copy
    r.pos.x = 0; # Modify by reference
    a @= r2; # Overwrite the value at the address, using the type of r2
    r = r2; # Same as the previous line, through the reference.

    # Incorrect, a reference is not an address:
    # r @= r2; # This would interpret the rect8 as an address.
    
    # Incorrect, the literal has no type associated with it:
    # a @= 0;
    # Correct:
    a @= 0 as rect8;

    # Technically valid; an address is just a number, and the left hand side of
    # `@=` is interpreted as an address. Write to a fixed memory location:
    $8000 @= 7 as byte;
    # Alternatively:
    @byte some_register is $8000;
    some_register = 7;

    # Pointer math works too, but use caution:
    a + $100 @= 0 as rect8;
end

void by_ref_example_2(rect8 r) # passed by value
    # by_ref_example_1(r) # Incorrect: interprets the bits of r as an address
    by_ref_example_1(@r) # Correct: pass the address of r
    address a = @r;
    by_ref_example_1(a); # Also correct
    @rect8 r2 is @r; # Valid but not useful
    by_ref_example_1(@r2) # Correct: recover the address of a reference
    # by_ref_example_1(r2) # Incorrect for the same reason as above
end

# Function definitions may be preceded by the keyword `inline`. Unlike C, this
# is not a suggestion. Inline functions are always expanded at their call sites:

inline void add_to_count(byte val)
    count = count + val;
end

# This expands to the exact same code as `increase_count` above
void also_increase_count(byte val)
    add_to_count(val);
end

# Function definitions may be nested as long as any nested functions are inline.
# Nested functions may refer to variables that are in-scope in their containing
# function:

byte conditional_add(bit sel0, bit sel1, byte a, byte b)
    inline byte a_or_b(bit flag)
        if (flag == 0)
            return a;
        end
        return b;
    end
    return a_or_b(sel0) + a_or_b(sel1);
end

# There are some limitations; inline functions cannot be recursive, cannot be
# forward declared, and you cannot take their address.

# The entrypoint of a program is the function `void main()`, which must exist.
# Functions can be forward-declared at the top level by putting a semicolon
# after the arguments instead of the function body: `void foo();`. 